  "prediction": "**Rare Nirvana Photos and Web Preservation Efforts Make Headlines**\n\nIn recent news, rare photographs from Nirvana's first concert in March 1987 have been unearthed and shared by Maggie Poukkula, the 19-year-old daughter of Tony Poukkula, a member of Seattle band Laytem. The photos, which feature Kurt Cobain and the band performing in Raymond, Washington, were taken by Poukkula's father during the band's early days. The discovery comes as part of a growing trend of rare Nirvana artifacts being shared, including those featured in the acclaimed documentary \"Montage of Heck.\"\n\nThe documentary, directed by Brett Morgen, has been well-received on the festival circuit and is set to return to movie theaters on August 7th. The film features a wealth of previously unseen footage and audio recordings of Cobain, offering a unique glimpse into the life and career of the iconic musician.\n\nIn other news, the Internet Archive is working to preserve web pages and fix broken links across the internet. The organization is using crawlers to archive pages as they are created and referencing the versions that were live when the page was written. This effort aims to preserve the original intent of a page's authors and provide a link to the archived version when a referenced page is changed or removed.\n\nThe Internet Archive's \"No More 404\" initiative is working to fix all broken links on the web, ensuring that users can access the information they need even when a page is no longer available. This effort is part of a broader push to preserve the internet's history and make it more accessible to future generations.\n\nOverall, these stories highlight the importance of preserving our cultural heritage and making it accessible to the public. Whether it's rare photographs of Nirvana or the efforts of the Internet Archive, these initiatives are working to ensure that our collective history is preserved for years to come.",
